<!--BlockML Documentation — a guide to BlockML's four ValueType kinds

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.TypeSystem-->
<docs:TypeSystem xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A guide to BlockML's four ValueType kinds
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: type system overview and the four ValueType kinds and how they relate to Blocks and
    members, grounded in org.blockml.bml.documentation.TypeSystemOverview. Body content is
    authored in a03 from that source.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="doc:TypeSystemOverview" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — PrimitiveType, StructType, EnumType, ConstantType as sibling branches.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:TypeExpressions" relation="ReadNext">
      Next reading step — multiplicity, unions, and other type-expression forms.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:ContractsModel" relation="SeeAlso">
      baseType versus contracts in the wider type system.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>The Type System</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Overview</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Guide</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <dpage:Section heading="Four sibling kinds under ValueType">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>BlockML's type system defines four sibling kinds of ValueType —
PrimitiveType, StructType, EnumType, and ConstantType — each naming a distinct
kind of semantic meaning that a Property or Capability type can carry. The four
kinds sit side by side under ValueType rather than forming a chain of
increasingly specific types, and none of them stores a concrete value itself —
only the Property that declares the type does.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Four ways to shape a value">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Each kind shapes a value differently: an atomic scalar, a set of named
fields, a closed set of named values, or a single fixed literal.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
            <dpage:List>
              <items>
                <dpage:ListItem text="PrimitiveType describes atomic scalar semantics with no internal structure, such as a whole number or a piece of text." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="StructType describes a composite value built from named fields, each field itself typed." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="EnumType describes a closed, finite set of named values that a Property may hold." />
                <dpage:ListItem text="ConstantType describes a single, fixed literal value, typically used as one alternative in a union." />
              </items>
            </dpage:List>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="How the kinds relate to Blocks and members">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A Property or Capability type must always be one of these four ValueType
kinds. The type describes what a value may look like, while the value itself
always lives on the owning Property and never on the type.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Classifying a type you encounter">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Given any type name, a reader can first ask which of the four sibling
kinds it belongs to before asking anything more specific about that kind.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with Primitive Types">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="Primitive Types" summary="Go deeper on the atomic scalar kind, the first of the four ValueType kinds.">
                  <target>docs:PrimitiveTypes</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:TypeSystem>
